The pattern, executed:

```go
func TestDouble(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name string
        in   int
        want int
    }{
        {"positive", 5, 10},
        {"zero", 0, 0},
        {"negative", -3, -6},
    }
    for _, tc := range tests {
        t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            if double(tc.in) != tc.want {
                t.Errorf("double(%d) = %d, want %d", tc.in, double(tc.in), tc.want)
            }
        })
    }
}
```

Executed output: each subtest named and passed:
```
=== RUN   TestDouble/positive
=== RUN   TestDouble/zero
=== RUN   TestDouble/negative
--- PASS: TestDouble (with subtests)
```

The advantages that made it THE Go pattern:
1. Add a case = add one struct literal — no copy-paste of test functions
2. t.Run creates SUBTESTS — each case reports separately; the failure tells you WHICH case (TestDouble/negative FAILED)
3. Run a single case: go test -run TestDouble/negative
4. The test data and the assertion logic are SEPARATE — the table is the specification

The conventions: a `name` field for readability; the anonymous struct in the function (or a named one for reuse); t.Run always (not a plain for loop — the subtest isolation is the point).
